1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
Welcome to practicals nine and ten:

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:05,558
bivariate analysis in Python.

3
00:00:05,558 --> 00:00:15,720
We build a correlation matrix, heatmaps and cross-tabs, and generate hypotheses, using the Superstore orders dataset.

4
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:22,415
First load the dataset and import pandas, numpy, seaborn and matplotlib.

5
00:00:22,415 --> 00:00:30,848
We use the latin one encoding to handle special characters, and drop the few rows with a blank segment.

6
00:00:31,648 --> 00:00:35,429
Next identify numeric and categorical features.

7
00:00:35,429 --> 00:00:47,979
Select dtypes with include number picks every numeric column in one command; then we drop postal code, because it is a number but an I D, not a measurement.

8
00:00:47,979 --> 00:00:52,887
The numeric columns are sales, quantity, discount and profit.

9
00:00:52,887 --> 00:00:58,840
For categoricals we choose columns with a manageable number of categories:

10
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:03,184
ship mode, segment, region, category and sub-category.

11
00:01:03,984 --> 00:01:11,246
One command, cov, computes covariance between every pair of the four numeric features at once.

12
00:01:11,246 --> 00:01:12,559
Notice the units:

13
00:01:12,559 --> 00:01:27,702
the covariance of sales and profit is about seventy thousand, and the sign is meaningful, but the huge number is not comparable to the covariance of discount and profit, about minus ten point six.

14
00:01:27,702 --> 00:01:30,792
That is why we compute correlation next.

15
00:01:31,592 --> 00:01:37,221
Correlation by default is Pearson; pass method equals spearman for ranks.

16
00:01:37,221 --> 00:01:42,001
Sales and profit are moderate positive, about zero point five.

17
00:01:42,001 --> 00:01:44,854
Sales and quantity are weak positive.

18
00:01:44,854 --> 00:01:53,567
And discount with profit jumps from minus zero point two two to minus zero point five four once we rank the data:

19
00:01:53,567 --> 00:02:00,584
a few heavily discounted, deeply negative-profit orders were flattening the Pearson number.

20
00:02:01,384 --> 00:02:05,877
A heatmap of the correlation matrix is one seaborn call.

21
00:02:05,877 --> 00:02:15,424
Annot true prints each value, and fixing the colour scale from minus one to plus one makes intensity always comparable.

22
00:02:16,224 --> 00:02:21,767
A scatterplot shows one pair and the shape a correlation number can hide.

23
00:02:21,767 --> 00:02:33,384
A pairplot draws a scatterplot for every pair of numeric features, plus a histogram per feature on the diagonal, covering all pairs at once with no loop.

24
00:02:34,184 --> 00:02:39,890
Pandas crosstab counts orders in every segment by ship mode combination.

25
00:02:39,890 --> 00:02:48,369
Normalize equals index turns each row into percentages, comparing ship-mode mix within each segment fairly.

26
00:02:48,369 --> 00:02:58,592
All three segments cluster near fifty nine to sixty percent standard class, so shipping preference looks similar across segments.

27
00:02:59,392 --> 00:03:02,862
The same shading idea applies to a cross-tab:

28
00:03:02,862 --> 00:03:04,481
pass it to a heatmap.

29
00:03:04,481 --> 00:03:13,888
And calling plot with kind bar and stacked true directly on a cross-tab turns counts into a stacked bar chart in one line.

30
00:03:14,688 --> 00:03:26,021
For categorical against numerical, group by category and take the mean of all four numeric columns in one line, or aggregate mean and standard deviation.

31
00:03:26,021 --> 00:03:35,280
Technology has the highest average profit despite the lowest average discount, while furniture has the lowest average profit.

32
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:41,676
A boxplot of profit by category shows the full distribution per group.

33
00:03:41,676 --> 00:03:48,152
A bar plot with the mean estimator compares average sales by segment at a glance.

34
00:03:48,952 --> 00:03:52,030
Now turn findings into hypotheses.

35
00:03:52,030 --> 00:03:52,573
H one:

36
00:03:52,573 --> 00:04:01,807
discount hurts profit, with Spearman r of minus zero point five four, strongest in the Central region.

37
00:04:01,807 --> 00:04:02,350
H two:

38
00:04:02,350 --> 00:04:07,148
category drives profitability more than sales volume.

39
00:04:07,148 --> 00:04:07,872
H three:

40
00:04:07,872 --> 00:04:12,941
ship-mode preference is independent of customer segment.

41
00:04:12,941 --> 00:04:13,575
H four:

42
00:04:13,575 --> 00:04:20,908
regional discounting policy varies, a policy effect and not a product-mix effect.

43
00:04:20,908 --> 00:04:25,072
These are hypotheses to test, not conclusions.

44
00:04:25,872 --> 00:04:30,803
The same six commands work on a different dataset, Titanic.

45
00:04:30,803 --> 00:04:44,343
Correlation of age, fare and family counts; a cross-tab of sex by survived with row percentages; grouped means by passenger class; and a boxplot of fare by class.

46
00:04:44,343 --> 00:04:54,289
Female passengers survived at a far higher rate than male passengers, and first-class passengers paid far higher fares.

47
00:04:54,289 --> 00:04:56,880
Same pattern, new column names.

48
00:04:57,680 --> 00:04:59,795
Your code cheat sheet.

49
00:04:59,795 --> 00:05:02,199
Numerical with numerical:

50
00:05:02,199 --> 00:05:08,833
cov, corr with optional Spearman, a heatmap, scatterplot or pairplot.

51
00:05:08,833 --> 00:05:11,622
Categorical with categorical:

52
00:05:11,622 --> 00:05:16,429
crosstab with normalize, a heatmap, a stacked bar.

53
00:05:16,429 --> 00:05:19,025
Categorical with numerical:

54
00:05:19,025 --> 00:05:23,929
group by with mean or aggregate, boxplot, bar plot.

55
00:05:23,929 --> 00:05:26,813
Try these on your own dataset.

56
00:05:26,813 --> 00:05:28,736
Thanks for watching.

